            <ArticleTitle>Relationship among body mass index, NT-proBNP, and mortality in decompensated chronic heart failure.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>172-177</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0147-9563(17)30017-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hrtlng.2017.01.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Obesity has been suggested to confer a survival benefit in acute heart failure. The concentrations of NT-proBNP may be reduced in patients with high body mass index (BMI).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the relationship among BMI, NT-proBNP, and mortality risk in decompensated chronic heart failure (DCHF).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This was a retrospective study. We studied 1001 patients with DCHF. Hazard ratios (HR) were calculated with Cox regression analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During the 1-year follow-up, 295 patients died. Compared with normal-weight patients, the unadjusted HR for death were 1.02 (95% CIs 0.79-1.33; p = 0.862) for patients with a BMI of 25.0-29.9 kg/m(2) and 0.83 (95% CIs 0.61-1.12; p = 0.213) for patients with a BMI ≥ 30 kg/m(2). NT-proBNP remained independently associated with mortality across the BMI categories. There was no statistically significant interaction between BMI and NT-proBNP levels for risk prediction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Obesity was not associated with mortality risk. NT-proBNP remained an independent prognostic factor across the BMI categories.</AbstractText>
